       i=$((i + 1))[m
[32m+[m[32m      [ ${#o} -le $w ] || o="${o:0:$((w - 1))}…"[m
       if [ $i = "$cur" ]; then printf '\r\033[K  \033[36m❯ %s\033[0m\n' "$o" >&2; else printf '\r\033[K    \033[2m%s\033[0m\n' "$o" >&2; fi[m
     done[m
     IFS= read -rsn1 key[m

[33mcommit 16f2d7c3c30136af08bbd40839a4578c6181d109[m
Author: Jane Doe <jane@example.com>  
Date:   Fri Oct 30 18:49:46 2026 +0300

    install.sh: arrow-key menu, checked answers, a summary, quiet steps; fix host-init key
    
    host-init.sh passed the public key to the remote script unquoted, so ssh split it at
    its spaces: authorized_keys got only "ssh-ed25519", the hostname became the key, and
    the new user could never log in. Every install on your own server failed there.
    
    install.sh now:
    - picks where FEMME runs with the arrow keys (numbers when not on a terminal);
    - asks for the sudo account up front, defaults it from IP, saves it as ADMIN, and
      checks it logs in (ssh-copy-id once for password-only VPSs);
    - rejects malformed aliases and email addresses (a keyboard escape slipped into one);
    - finds the latest herdr release instead of asking;
    - defaults the board email to git's user.email;
    - shows what it is about to set up and asks before touching a server;
    - runs the long steps behind a spinner, the output in a log printed on failure;
    - tells you to run ./install.sh update after signing in to Claude, so cswap
      (the board's accounts window) gets set up.
    
    host-init.sh says it could not log in, instead of blaming the OS, when ssh fails.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

